## 2. Ethical Data Collection Methodologies for Neurodiverse Populations

### 2.1 Ethical Principles Table

| Principle                       | Description                                                                         | Implementation Guide                                    |
|----------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------|
| Informed Consent & Comprehension| Ensure understanding and ongoing consent at every system interaction                | Multi-stage, plain-language consent with regular check-ins |
| Privacy & Anonymity             | Protect identities and sensitive data throughout lifecycle                          | Anonymization, encryption, local storage, zero-trust design |
| Representation & Inclusivity     | Proactively gather diverse, intersectional neurodivergent voices                    | Outreach through forums, participatory research, diversity audits |
| Lived Experience Prioritization | Center real-world narratives and feedback over abstracted clinical assessments      | Narrative-based inputs, optional qualitative modules        |
| Community Oversight and Feedback| Enable participatory review, system audit, and policy revision                     | Community boards, in-platform polls, transparent change logs |

Comprehensive consent must go beyond a checkbox. Explanations should use clear, everyday language, provide context for data use, and adapt for cognitive flexibility (audio, video, summary, etc.). Withdrawal of consent, deletion of data, and review of usage logs must be “one-click” accessible, supported by ongoing, transparent communication.

### 2.2 Collection Techniques

- **Multi-Modal Interfaces**: Offer interaction through voice, text, visuals, and alternative input devices to accommodate sensory, executive, or linguistic differences.
- **Narrative & Story-Based Inputs**: Encourage self-authored narrative, artifact sharing, or collaborative interviews to gather nuanced, real-life accounts.
- **Community-Led Outreach & Recruitment**: Use neurodivergent organizations, advocacy groups, and neurodiverse hiring programs for direct recruitment, incentivizing squads to contribute and review data .
- **Dynamic Questionnaires & Qualitative Methods**: Adapt question types and session structure in real time based on user’s processing, pacing, or fatigue signals.
- **Consent Stewardship**: Implement versioned consent forms, configurable scopes (what data, which modules, sharing limits), and notification systems for all changes to data usage.

**Participatory Action Research** and community-driven AI are vital; individuals must retain power to direct data collection and system evolution—a core lesson from recent research and policy.

